(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-i-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies I Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where is acute lymphoblastic leukemia typically found, in contrast to an aggressive lymphoma?","choices":["Spleen with fibrosis","Bone with M protein","Diffuse marrow and blood","Nodes with little marrow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia often shows diffuse marrow and peripheral blood involvement, in contrast to aggressive lymphomas, which involve nodal or extranodal sites with minimal bone marrow involvement. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 25, 53, 16"},{"q":"Which age groups carry the highest risk of acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","choices":["Adults over 60","Adults near 70","Adults 45 to 55","Under 5 and over 50"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia has a bimodal distribution: risk is highest in children under 5 and adults over 50. It is the most common malignancy under age 15, and in adults incidence peaks around 50. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 21"},{"q":"For Philadelphia chromosome-negative acute lymphoblastic leukemia, pediatric-like regimens are usually given below what age?","choices":["15 years","40 years","60 years","70 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For Philadelphia chromosome-negative disease, pediatric-like regimens are usually given to patients younger than 40: intensive chemotherapy with induction, consolidation and maintenance phases. In older adults, therapy changes may be needed for tolerance. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which treatment lowers the red cell excess in polycythemia vera?","choices":["Arsenic trioxide","Phlebotomy","Red cell transfusion","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of polycythemia vera is phlebotomy, which removes red cells and so lowers the increased blood viscosity, with hydroxyurea as the drug option. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 48, 52"},{"q":"Which viral infection is listed as a risk factor for acute myeloid leukemia?","choices":["Human immunodeficiency virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Human T-lymphotropic virus 1","Pertussis infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The viral infection listed for acute myeloid leukemia is human immunodeficiency virus, alongside prior high-dose radiation, benzene, petroleum, alkylating chemotherapy, Down syndrome, Fanconi anemia, age over 60 and male sex. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 21, 24, 34"},{"q":"Which chemical exposure is named as a risk factor for hematologic malignancies?","choices":["Benzene","Arsenic","Hydroxyurea","Aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Environmental risk factors include radiation and specific chemical exposures such as benzene, alongside genetic factors, autoimmune disorders and older age. Benzene is listed again for both acute lymphoblastic and acute myeloid leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 21, 34"},{"q":"Before tyrosine kinase inhibitors, chronic myelogenous leukemia historically progressed to acute leukemia in about how many years?","choices":["1-2 years","4-6 years","10-15 years","14 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic myelogenous leukemia historically progressed from a chronic condition to acute leukemia in about 4-6 years and was considered fatal; with tyrosine kinase inhibitors it is now largely a chronic disorder with long-term survival. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How often does polycythemia vera transform to leukemia?","choices":["About 5%","About 25%","About 50%","Over 80%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polycythemia vera has an excellent prognosis, with an estimated survival of 14 years, and rarely results in leukemic transformation, in about 5% of cases. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 49, 52, 63"},{"q":"In acute lymphoblastic leukemia, when is allogeneic stem cell transplant used?","choices":["Adverse-risk or relapsed disease","Hyperdiploid childhood disease","In place of induction","Every adult at diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Allogeneic stem cell transplant is used where feasible for adverse-risk acute lymphoblastic leukemia or relapsed disease. Most patients start with intensive chemotherapy, with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor added if Philadelphia-positive. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which approach is used for fit patients with acute myeloid leukemia?","choices":["Arsenic and retinoic acid","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor","Intensive chemotherapy (7+3)","Hypomethylating agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fit patients receive intensive chemotherapy, often cytarabine plus an anthracycline (7+3). Allogeneic stem cell transplant typically follows for most intermediate- and high-risk patients who achieve remission. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which test separates acute lymphoblastic leukemia from the atypical lymphocytosis of mononucleosis or pertussis?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Chest X-ray","Coagulation panel","Flow cytometry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Some infections, including mononucleosis and pertussis, cause atypical lymphocytosis, and flow cytometry differentiates these from acute lymphoblastic leukemia based on its clonal nature. It also shows whether the leukemia is B-cell or T-cell. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 23, 24"},{"q":"Why can essential thrombocythemia cause bleeding despite a high platelet count?","choices":["Acquired von Willebrand factor loss","Low platelets from marrow failure","Disseminated coagulopathy","Portal hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding with high platelets can occur in essential thrombocythemia because of acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency, leaving a shortage of factor at the site of injury. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"When myeloid growth factors are given for neutropenia in myelodysplastic syndrome, what is the caution?","choices":["Triggering coagulopathy","Causing marrow fibrosis","Overstimulating the blasts","Raising blood viscosity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia in myelodysplastic syndrome may be treated with myeloid growth factors, but with care not to cause overstimulation of blasts. The disease already sits close to acute myeloid leukemia at the high-risk end. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which finding favors a reactive leukocytosis over chronic myelogenous leukemia?","choices":["Positive fusion gene by polymerase chain reaction","Platelet count above normal with anemia","Abdominal discomfort from a large spleen","White count under 50,000, no splenomegaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A reactive leukocytosis from infection has no splenomegaly, a white count usually below 50,000, and a negative BCR-ABL1 (breakpoint cluster region-Abelson 1) result. Chronic myelogenous leukemia counts are often above 100,000. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 59, 61"},{"q":"Which lab pattern fits chronic-phase chronic myelogenous leukemia?","choices":["Pancytopenia, dysplasia","Blasts of 20% or more","Raised hematocrit","High white cells and platelets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Labs show marked leukocytosis, often above 100,000 and up to 500,000, thrombocytosis and anemia, with usually very low blasts unless the disease is in accelerated or blast phase. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 59, 61, 62"},{"q":"Which treatment is unique to acute promyelocytic leukemia?","choices":["Cytarabine plus anthracycline","Hypomethylating agent","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor","Arsenic and retinoic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute promyelocytic leukemia has a unique treatment approach: arsenic trioxide and all-trans retinoic acid, rather than the chemotherapy used for other acute myeloid leukemia. Patients who survive the presentation have a cure rate above 90%. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which test is required in almost every hematologic malignancy to secure the correct diagnosis?","choices":["Complete blood count alone","Chemistry panel","Lumbar puncture","Marrow biopsy or flow cytometry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Almost all hematologic malignancies require a bone marrow biopsy and/or flow cytometry as part of the work-up to ensure the correct diagnosis. Blood counts and chemistry panels may raise the suspicion but do not settle it. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 25"},{"q":"Which transplant is also called stem cell rescue?","choices":["Red cell transfusion","Leukapheresis","Autologous transplant","Allogeneic transplant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An autologous stem cell transplant uses the person's own stem cells as part of the transplantation process, often to rescue the marrow after high-dose chemotherapy, hence the name stem cell rescue. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 12, 35"},{"q":"Blasts at or above what percentage turn higher-risk myelodysplastic syndrome into acute myeloid leukemia?","choices":["5%","10%","20%","30%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Higher-risk myelodysplastic syndrome may have blasts, but below 20%; at 20% or more the diagnosis is acute myeloid leukemia. Very high-risk myelodysplastic syndrome with elevated blasts overlaps substantially with acute myeloid leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 38, 47"},{"q":"Hematologic malignancies result from mutation in and proliferation of what?","choices":["Several clones at once","Inherited germline cells","Reactive immune cells","A single clone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hematologic malignancies are cancers of blood-forming tissues or the lymphatic system arising from mutation in and proliferation of a hematologic clone, so they are monoclonal. The first oncogenic mutation occurs in a single somatic cell, which then gains a growth advantage. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 24"},{"q":"About what is the overall five-year survival of acute myeloid leukemia?","choices":["5%","25-30%","48%","90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment brings initial remission in many patients, but acute myeloid leukemia frequently relapses and treatment-related mortality is high: overall survival is only 48% at one year and about 25-30% at five years. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What do patients with chronic myelogenous leukemia need to know about how long treatment lasts?","choices":["Until the spleen shrinks","Lifelong unless sustained response","A fixed course of 2-5 years","Until counts first normalize"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment is required for life unless the patient achieves a sustained major molecular remission, at which time discontinuation is possible. Many patients live a full life with no detriment to long-term survival if appropriately managed. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 28, 66"},{"q":"What is the hallmark therapy for chronic-phase chronic myelogenous leukemia?","choices":["Cytarabine-based 7+3","Janus kinase inhibitors","Tyrosine kinase inhibitors","Phlebotomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tyrosine kinase inhibitors are the hallmark of therapy for chronic-phase chronic myelogenous leukemia, with six approved agents. They turned a once-fatal leukemia into a largely chronic disorder with long-term survival. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 56, 63"},{"q":"Multiple myeloma arises from which cell?","choices":["Plasma cells","T cells","Myeloid progenitors","Lymphoblasts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multiple myeloma arises from terminally differentiated B cells called plasma cells. Because plasma cells make our antibodies, myeloma produces an excess of a single monoclonal antibody, the M protein. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the median age at diagnosis of chronic myelogenous leukemia?","choices":["Under 15 years","45-55 years","Over 60 years","About 70 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The median age of chronic myelogenous leukemia is 45-55 years; it may occur in either sex but is slightly more common in men. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 31, 46, 58"},{"q":"What is the only curative therapy for myelodysplastic syndrome?","choices":["Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Hypomethylating agents","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents","Autologous stem cell transplant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The only curative therapy for myelodysplastic syndrome is allogeneic stem cell transplant, using a healthy donor's stem cells, but it is high risk and has a limited role in therapy. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 12, 48"},{"q":"In which group is acute myeloid leukemia mostly seen?","choices":["Adults over 60","Children under 5","Adolescents","Adults aged 45 to 55"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute myeloid leukemia is mostly seen in adults, particularly older adults over 60, and age over 60 is listed among its risk factors. Its incidence in children is unusual outside of neonates. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 31, 34"},{"q":"In a hematologic malignancy, bleeding is usually due to which count abnormality?","choices":["Anemia","Neutropenia","Leukocytosis","Thrombocytopenia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hematologic malignancies often present with non-specific symptoms: fatigue from anemia, bleeding from thrombocytopenia, swollen nodes in lymphoma and bone pain in myeloma. Low platelets explain the bruising and bleeding. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 22"},{"q":"Which presentation is typical of myelofibrosis?","choices":["Gum hyperplasia","Early satiety from splenomegaly","Itching after warm showers","A mediastinal mass"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myelofibrosis presents non-specifically with fatigue and abdominal symptoms from splenomegaly, including pain, early satiety and bloating; night sweats, fever and weight loss are also common. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Acute myeloid leukemia has historically been defined by myeloid blasts at or above what percentage?","choices":["5%","10%","20%","30%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute myeloid leukemia has historically been defined by myeloid blasts at or above 20%; newer guidelines note that certain genetic mutations may allow the diagnosis with 10% or higher. Below 20%, a dysplastic marrow with blasts is myelodysplastic syndrome. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 38, 47"}]};
